<body>
<div class="ltx_page_main">
<div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Anand Deopurkar
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Anand Patel
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove that the rational Picard group of the simple Hurwitz space <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial for <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> up to five. We also relate the rational Picard groups of the Hurwitz spaces to the rational Picard groups of the Severi varieties of nodal curves on Hirzebruch surfaces.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S0" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">0 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S0.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S0.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the simple Hurwitz space which parametrizes isomorphism classes of simply branched degree <math id="S0.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> covers of genus zero curves by genus <math id="S0.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> curves. Although <math id="S0.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> has been studied classically, many fundamental questions about its geometry are still unanswered. The goal of this paper is to address one such question, the question of its Picard group. It is conjectured (for example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">DE96</a>]</cite>) that the rational Picard group <math id="S0.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}(\mathcal{H}_{d,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}(\mathcal{H}_{d,g})</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial. We call this the <em id="S0.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Picard rank conjecture</em> for <math id="S0.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our main result is a proof of this conjecture for <math id="S0.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmaintheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_maintheorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmmaintheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem A</span></span><span id="Thmmaintheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmmaintheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmaintheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmaintheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The rational Picard group of <math id="Thmmaintheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial for <math id="Thmmaintheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S0.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the main text, <a href="#Thmmaintheorem1" title="Theorem A. ‣ 0 Introduction ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> is divided into the case of degree 3 (<a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="3.3. ‣ 3 Degree three ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>), degree 4 (<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem10" title="4.10. ‣ 4 Degree four ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a>), and degree 5 (<a href="#S5.Thmtheorem4" title="5.4. ‣ 5 Degree five ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The Picard rank conjecture was known for <math id="S0.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S0.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S0.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2</annotation></semantics></math>, it was proved by Cornalba and Harris <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">CH88</a>, Lemma 4.5]</cite>, and for <math id="S0.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math> by Stankova-Frenkel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">SF00</a>, § 12.2]</cite>. In these cases, now there are more refined results about the Picard group of the moduli stacks; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cor07</a>]</cite> for <math id="S0.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">BV12</a>]</cite> for <math id="S0.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The conjecture is also known for <math id="S0.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;2g-2</annotation></semantics></math>. In this range, the map <math id="S0.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fibration, where <math id="S0.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is the moduli space of smooth curves of genus <math id="S0.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. An analysis of this fibration shows that <math id="S0.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}(\mathcal{H}_{d,g})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}(\mathcal{H}_{d,g})=0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S0.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}(\mathcal{M}_{g})\cong{\bf Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}(\mathcal{M}_{g})\cong{\bf Q}</annotation></semantics></math> (see, for example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moc95</a>]</cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">DE96</a>, § 3]</cite>). Thus, the conjecture for <math id="S0.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;2g-2</annotation></semantics></math> follows from Harer’s theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har83</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We briefly explain the rationale behind the conjecture. Let us blur the distinction between the coarse moduli spaces and the fine moduli stacks. This is harmless, since we are concerned with the rational Picard group. Let us also take <math id="S0.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> (the discussion holds for <math id="S0.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2,3</annotation></semantics></math> with minor modifications). Denote by <math id="S0.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> the partial compactification of <math id="S0.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> that parametrizes covers <math id="S0.p5.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S0.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is allowed to be nodal, but still irreducible, and <math id="S0.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> need not be simply branched. Let <math id="S0.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}\mathcal{C}\to\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal family over <math id="S0.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S0.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho{\colon}\mathcal{C}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho{\colon}\mathcal{C}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of irreducible, at worst nodal curves of arithmetic genus <math id="S0.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S0.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi{\colon}\mathcal{P}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi{\colon}\mathcal{P}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> a family of smooth curves of genus <math id="S0.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>. From this data, we can construct three ‘tautological’ divisor classes on <math id="S0.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> given by</p>
<table id="S0.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{\rho})^{2}),\quad\rho_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{\rho})\alpha^{*}c_{1}(\omega_{\pi})),\text{ and }\rho_{*}([\delta_{\rho}])." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{\rho})^{2}),\quad\rho_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{\rho})\alpha^{*}c_{1}(\omega_{\pi})),\text{ and }\rho_{*}([\delta_{\rho}]).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S0.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S0.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> stands for the relative dualizing sheaf and <math id="S0.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> for the singular locus. It is easy to check that the three tautological classes are <math id="S0.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-linearly independent. On the other hand, <math id="S0.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}\setminus\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}\setminus\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a union of three irreducible divisors, namely the locus <math id="S0.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S0.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is singular, the locus <math id="S0.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S0.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> has a higher order ramification point, and the locus <math id="S0.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S0.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> has two ramification points over a branch point. It is also easy to check that the classes of <math id="S0.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S0.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S0.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S0.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-linearly independent. Thus, <math id="S0.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}(\mathcal{H}_{d,g})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}(\mathcal{H}_{d,g})=0</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to <math id="S0.p5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}(\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}(\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g})</annotation></semantics></math> being generated by the tautological classes. The Picard rank conjecture thus expresses the often-satisfied expectation that there are no other divisor classes than the tautological ones.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We now outline our strategy for proving <a href="#Thmmaintheorem1" title="Theorem A. ‣ 0 Introduction ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. Let <math id="S0.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a degree <math id="S0.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> cover. Then <math id="S0.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> embeds in a <math id="S0.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle over <math id="S0.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we denote by <math id="S0.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}E\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}E\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thanks to the work of Casnati and Ekedahl, the resolution of the ideal of <math id="S0.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S0.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math> can be described explicitly. The terms in this resolution involve (twists of) vector bundles on <math id="S0.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">CE96</a>]</cite>. Let <math id="S0.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the open locus where these vector bundles are the most generic. The key steps in our proof are the following.</p>
<ol id="S0.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S0.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S0.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Identify the divisorial components of <math id="S0.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}\setminus U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}\setminus U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S0.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S0.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Express <math id="S0.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> as a (successive) quotient of an open subset of an affine space by actions of linear algebraic groups.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S0.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S0.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Use the previous two steps to get a bound on the Picard rank of <math id="S0.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>, and in turn, the Picard rank of <math id="S0.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S0.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Needless to say, we are able to carry out all three steps only for <math id="S0.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>. However, we can carry out parts of step (1) in general. For
step (2), we highlight that it remains unknown in general whether one
can dominate <math id="S0.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> by an affine space for <math id="S0.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 6</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze <math id="S0.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}\setminus U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}\setminus U</annotation></semantics></math>, we must analyze the loci in
<math id="S0.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> where the bundle <math id="S0.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and the vector bundles appearing
in the resolution of <math id="S0.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> are unbalanced. We call these loci the Maroni
loci and the Casnati–Ekedahl loci, respectively. We spend significant
effort on understanding the decomposition of <math id="S0.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> into these
loci. Contained in <a href="#S2" title="2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section 2</span></a>, the results of this analysis may
be of independent interest.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.p8.1" class="ltx_p">A key tool in our analysis is a construction that relates the Maroni
loci to the Severi varieties of Hirzebruch surfaces. Originally due to
Ohbuchi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ohb97</a>]</cite>, this ‘associated scroll
construction’ allows us to get the required dimension estimates. The
key input here is a theorem of Tyomkin that guarantees that the Severi
varieties of Hirzebruch surfaces are irreducible of the expected
dimension <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tyo07</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S0.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The associated scroll construction also lets us relate the Picard
ranks of the Hurwitz spaces to the Picard ranks of the Severi
varieties. To state our result, let us denote by <math id="S0.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> the
space of irreducible nodal curves of geometric genus <math id="S0.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> in the linear
system <math id="S0.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d\tau|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d\tau|</annotation></semantics></math> on the Hirzebruch surface <math id="S0.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S0.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is the
section with self-intersection <math id="S0.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmaintheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_maintheorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmmaintheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem B</span></span><span id="Thmmaintheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmmaintheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmaintheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmaintheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmmaintheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq\lfloor(g+d-1)/(d-1)\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq\lfloor(g+d-1)/(d-1)\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="Thmmaintheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\mathcal{U}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\mathcal{U}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)=0</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="Thmmaintheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\mathcal{H}_{d,g}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\mathcal{H}_{d,g}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmmaintheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmmaintheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmmaintheorem2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmmaintheorem2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq\lceil 2(g+d-1)/(d-1)\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq\lceil 2(g+d-1)/(d-1)\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="Thmmaintheorem2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\mathcal{U}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\mathcal{U}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)=0</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="Thmmaintheorem2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\mathcal{H}_{d,g}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\mathcal{H}_{d,g}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S0.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In the main text, <a href="#Thmmaintheorem2" title="Theorem B. ‣ 0 Introduction ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> is <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem7" title="6.7. ‣ 6 From Hurwitz spaces to Severi varieties ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem 6.7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S0.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">0.1 </span>Notation</h3>

<div id="S0.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We work with a few different versions of the Hurwitz spaces. We assemble their definitions here. We work over the field <math id="S0.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf C}</annotation></semantics></math> of complex numbers. By a curve, we mean a connected, proper, reduced scheme of finite type over <math id="S0.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf C}</annotation></semantics></math>. Throughout, assume that <math id="S0.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<dl id="S0.I2" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S0.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S0.I2.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S0.I2.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S0.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the coarse moduli space of <math id="S0.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S0.I2.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth curve of genus <math id="S0.I2.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S0.I2.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> a finite map of degree <math id="S0.I2.ix1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> with simple branching (that is, the branch divisor of <math id="S0.I2.ix1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is supported at <math id="S0.I2.ix1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+2d-2</annotation></semantics></math> distinct points). Two such covers <math id="S0.I2.ix1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha_{1}{\colon}C_{1}\to{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha_{1}{\colon}C_{1}\to{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S0.I2.ix1.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha_{2}{\colon}C_{2}\to{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha_{2}{\colon}C_{2}\to{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> are considered isomorphic if there are isomorphisms <math id="S0.I2.ix1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi{\colon}C_{1}\to C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi{\colon}C_{1}\to C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S0.I2.ix1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi{\colon}{\bf P}^{1}\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi{\colon}{\bf P}^{1}\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S0.I2.ix1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{2}\circ\phi=\psi\circ\alpha_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{2}\circ\phi=\psi\circ\alpha_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S0.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S0.I2.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S0.I2.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S0.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the coarse moduli space of <math id="S0.I2.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S0.I2.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is an irreducible, at worst nodal curve of arithmetic genus <math id="S0.I2.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S0.I2.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> a finite map of degree <math id="S0.I2.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. The isomorphism condition is the same as that for <math id="S0.I2.ix2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S0.I2.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S0.I2.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S0.I2.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S0.I2.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.I2.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is like <math id="S0.I2.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>, but with ‘framed’ target <math id="S0.I2.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The objects it parametrizes are <math id="S0.I2.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> as in the description of <math id="S0.I2.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>, but <math id="S0.I2.ix3.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha_{1}{\colon}C_{1}\to{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha_{1}{\colon}C_{1}\to{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S0.I2.ix3.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha_{2}{\colon}C_{2}\to{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha_{2}{\colon}C_{2}\to{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> are considered isomorphic if there is an isomorphism <math id="S0.I2.ix3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi{\colon}C_{1}\to C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi{\colon}C_{1}\to C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S0.I2.ix3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{2}\circ\phi=\alpha_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{2}\circ\phi=\alpha_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S0.I2.ix4" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S0.I2.ix4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S0.I2.ix4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S0.I2.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.I2.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is like <math id="S0.I2.ix4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>, but with framed target <math id="S0.I2.ix4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
<p id="S0.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">All four are irreducible quasi-projective varieties with at worst quotient singularities. In particular, they are normal and <math id="S0.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-factorial. The group <math id="S0.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Aut}{\bf P}^{1}=\operatorname{PGL}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Aut}{\bf P}^{1}=\operatorname{PGL}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on the framed versions. The unframed versions are the quotients by this action in the sense that the fibers of the morphism from the framed space to the unframed space are precisely the <math id="S0.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{PGL}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{PGL}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> orbits. We have</p>
<table id="S0.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g}=\dim\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}=2g+2d-5," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g}=\dim\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}=2g+2d-5,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S0.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="S0.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S0.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g}^{\dagger}=\dim\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}=2g+2d-2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\mathcal{H}_{d,g}^{\dagger}=\dim\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}=2g+2d-2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S0.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, we work with the following Severi varieties:</p>
<dl id="S0.I3" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S0.I3.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S0.I3.ix1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S0.I3.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S0.I3.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.I3.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the locus of irreducible nodal curves of geometric genus <math id="S0.I3.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> in the linear series <math id="S0.I3.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d\tau|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d\tau|</annotation></semantics></math> in the Hirzebruch surface <math id="S0.I3.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S0.I3.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\subset{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\subset{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is the section of self-intersection <math id="S0.I3.ix1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S0.I3.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S0.I3.ix2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S0.I3.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S0.I3.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.I3.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the closure of <math id="S0.I3.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> in the projective space <math id="S0.I3.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d\tau|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d\tau|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S0.I3.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><math id="S0.I3.ix3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\rm irr}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>irr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\rm irr}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S0.I3.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S0.I3.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.I3.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is the open subset of reduced and irreducible curves in <math id="S0.I3.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
<div id="S0.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S0.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We do not distinguish between a vector bundle and the corresponding locally free sheaf. Note that the vector bundle associated to the locally free sheaf <math id="S0.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is the relative <math id="S0.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Spec}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Spec</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Spec}</annotation></semantics></math> of the symmetric algebra on <math id="S0.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this expository section, we recall two key results. The first describes the Picard group of the quotient of a variety by a group action. The second is a structure theorem for finite covers which enables us to describe a large open subset of the Hurwitz space as such a quotient.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Picard groups of quotients</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a linear algebraic group acting on a variety <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{G}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{G}X</annotation></semantics></math> the group of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-linearized line bundles on <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>. Forgetting the <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-linearization gives a homomorphism <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{G}X\to\operatorname{Pic}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{G}X\to\operatorname{Pic}X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">KKV89</a><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Lemma 2.2 + Proposition 2.3]</span></cite><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> For a connected linear algebraic group <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> acting on an irreducible variety <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, we have an exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(G)\to\operatorname{Pic}_{G}X\to\operatorname{Pic}X," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(G)\to\operatorname{Pic}_{G}X\to\operatorname{Pic}X,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is the group of (algebraic) characters of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, if <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is normal, then the sequence has an extension by a homomorphism <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}X\to\operatorname{Pic}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}X\to\operatorname{Pic}G</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi{\colon}X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi{\colon}X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> be a morphism that is equivariant with the trivial <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> action on <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> be a line bundle on <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. The pullback <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}L</annotation></semantics></math> carries a natural <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-linearization. We thus have a homomorphism <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}Y\to\operatorname{Pic}_{G}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}Y\to\operatorname{Pic}_{G}X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be irreducible normal varieties, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> a linear algebraic group acting on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi{\colon}X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi{\colon}X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> a surjective morphism, equivariant with the trivial action on <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose the fibers of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> consist of single <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-orbits. Then the map <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}Y\to\operatorname{Pic}_{G}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}Y\to\operatorname{Pic}_{G}X</annotation></semantics></math> is injective and we have</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}\operatorname{Pic}Y\leq\operatorname{rk}\chi(G)+\operatorname{rk}\operatorname{Pic}X." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}\operatorname{Pic}Y\leq\operatorname{rk}\chi(G)+\operatorname{rk}\operatorname{Pic}X.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore, if <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is reductive and the stabilizers <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> are finite, then we have an isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}Y\otimes{\bf Q}\xrightarrow{\sim}\operatorname{Pic}_{G}X\otimes{\bf Q}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝐐</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}Y\otimes{\bf Q}\xrightarrow{\sim}\operatorname{Pic}_{G}X\otimes{\bf Q}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is a line bundle on <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}L</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial as a <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-linearized line bundle. Then <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}L</annotation></semantics></math> has a <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant nowhere-vanishing section. We claim that such a section descends to a nowhere-vanishing section of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. The crucial point is that in our setup, <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is a geometric quotient of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">MFK94</a>, Proposition 0.2]</cite>. That is, for every open <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset Y</annotation></semantics></math>, the preimage <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}U</annotation></semantics></math> is open and the functions on <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> are the invariant functions on <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}U</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(U,O_{Y})=\Gamma(\pi^{-1}U,O_{X})^{G}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(U,O_{Y})=\Gamma(\pi^{-1}U,O_{X})^{G}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">It follows that the sections of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> are the invariant sections of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}L</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{-1}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{-1}(U)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(U,L)=\Gamma(\pi^{-1}U,\pi^{*}L)^{G}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(U,L)=\Gamma(\pi^{-1}U,\pi^{*}L)^{G}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">Thus, a <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant section <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}L</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> gives a section <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. It is easy to check that if <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is nowhere-vanishing, so is <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The bound on <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}\operatorname{Pic}Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}\operatorname{Pic}Y</annotation></semantics></math> follows from the injectivity and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="1.1. ‣ 1.1 Picard groups of quotients ‣ 1 Preliminaries ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>. For the last statement, we use the characterization of the image of <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}Y\to\operatorname{Pic}_{G}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}Y\to\operatorname{Pic}_{G}X</annotation></semantics></math> from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">KKV89</a>, Proposition 4.2]</cite>: a <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-linearized line bundle <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is in the image if and only if for every <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>, the stabilizer group <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> acts trivially on the fiber <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the stabilizers are finite, we can arrange this by passing to a large enough power of <math id="S1.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We end with a simple application.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> be any open subset and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>†</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> its preimage under <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}\to\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}\operatorname{Pic}U=\operatorname{rk}\operatorname{Pic}U^{\dagger}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Pic</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}\operatorname{Pic}U=\operatorname{rk}\operatorname{Pic}U^{\dagger}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Apply <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="1.1. ‣ 1.1 Picard groups of quotients ‣ 1 Preliminaries ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem2" title="1.2. ‣ 1.1 Picard groups of quotients ‣ 1 Preliminaries ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> with <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=\operatorname{PGL}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>PGL</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\operatorname{PGL}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=U^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>†</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=U^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=U</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> be integral schemes and <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> a finite flat Gorenstein morphism of degree <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. The map <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> gives an exact sequence</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to O_{Y}\to\alpha_{*}O_{X}\to{E_{\alpha}}^{\vee}\to 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>X</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mo>∨</mo></mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to O_{Y}\to\alpha_{*}O_{X}\to{E_{\alpha}}^{\vee}\to 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector bundle of rank <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, called the <em id="S1.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Tschirnhausen bundle</em> of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> the dualizing sheaf of <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Hom}_{Y}(-,O_{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Hom}_{Y}(-,O_{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> to (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1.2 The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ 1 Preliminaries ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>), we get</p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to E\to\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}\to O_{Y}\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to E\to\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}\to O_{Y}\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">The map <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\to\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\to\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a map <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*}E\to\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*}E\to\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">CE96</a><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Theorem 2.1]</span></cite><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
In the above setup, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha^{*}E\to\omega_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha^{*}E\to\omega_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> gives an embedding <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota{\colon}X\to{\bf P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ι</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota{\colon}X\to{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\pi\circ\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ι</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\pi\circ\iota</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi{\colon}{\bf P}E\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi{\colon}{\bf P}E\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is the projection. Moreover, the subscheme <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset{\bf P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math> can be described as follows.</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The resolution of </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{X}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as an </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{{\bf P}E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{{\bf P}E}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> module has the form</span></p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}0\to\pi^{*}N_{d-2}(-d)\to\pi^{*}N_{d-3}(-d+2)\to\pi^{*}N_{d-4}(-d+3)\to\dots{\\
}\dots\to\pi^{*}N_{2}(-3)\to\pi^{*}N_{1}(-2)\to O_{{\bf P}E}\to O_{X}\to 0,\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mi>
</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>O</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>O</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}0\to\pi^{*}N_{d-2}(-d)\to\pi^{*}N_{d-3}(-d+2)\to\pi^{*}N_{d-4}(-d+3)\to\dots{\\
}\dots\to\pi^{*}N_{2}(-3)\to\pi^{*}N_{1}(-2)\to O_{{\bf P}E}\to O_{X}\to 0,\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where the </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are vector bundles on </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Restricted to a point </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, this sequence is the minimal free resolution of </span><math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{y}\subset{\bf P}E_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{y}\subset{\bf P}E_{y}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The ranks of the </span><math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> are given by</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}N_{i}=\frac{i(d-2-i)}{d-1}{d\choose{i+1}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}N_{i}=\frac{i(d-2-i)}{d-1}{d\choose{i+1}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{d-2}\cong\pi^{*}\det E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{d-2}\cong\pi^{*}\det E</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Furthermore, the resolution is symmetric, that is, isomorphic to the resolution obtained by applying </span><math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Hom}_{O_{{\bf P}E}}(-,N_{d-2}(-d))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Hom</mi><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Hom}_{O_{{\bf P}E}}(-,N_{d-2}(-d))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The branch divisor of <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> is given by a section of <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\det E)^{\otimes 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\det E)^{\otimes 2}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, if <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a curve of (arithmetic) genus <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y={\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y={\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="S1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{rk}E=d-1\text{ and }\deg E=g+d-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{rk}E=d-1\text{ and }\deg E=g+d-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider a cover <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and the relative canonical embedding <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset{\bf P}E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset{\bf P}E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since vector bundles on <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> split as direct sums of line bundles, the vector bundle <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the higher syzygy bundles <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="1.4. ‣ 1.2 The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ 1 Preliminaries ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem 1.4</span></a> are discrete invariants of <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. We thus get a decomposition of the Hurwitz space into locally closed subsets where the isomorphism type of the bundles <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are constant. This section is devoted to the analysis of some of these locally closed subvarieties, particularly their dimensions. We only consider the bundle <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\alpha}:=N_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\alpha}:=N_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}=\ker(\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}\to O_{Y})\text{ and }F_{\alpha}=\alpha_{*}I_{C}(2)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}=\ker(\alpha_{*}\omega_{\alpha}\to O_{Y})\text{ and }F_{\alpha}=\alpha_{*}I_{C}(2),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{C}\subset O_{{\bf P}E_{\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{C}\subset O_{{\bf P}E_{\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the ideal sheaf of <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For vector bundles <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, define the following closed subvarieties of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M(E,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M(E,F)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle:=\overline{\{[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\mid E_{\alpha}\cong E\text{ and }F_{\alpha}\cong F\}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐏</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>E</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><mi>E</mi><mtext> and </mtext><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\overline{\{[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\mid E_{\alpha}\cong E\text{ and }F_{\alpha}\cong F\}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M(E)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle:=\overline{\{[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\mid E_{\alpha}\cong E\}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐏</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>E</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\overline{\{[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\mid E_{\alpha}\cong E\}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C(F)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle:=\overline{\{[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\mid F_{\alpha}\cong F\}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">:</mo><mo lspace="0em">=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐏</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\overline{\{[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\mid F_{\alpha}\cong F\}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Call <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(E)</annotation></semantics></math> the <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Maroni loci</em> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(F)</annotation></semantics></math> the <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Casnati–Ekedahl loci</em>. Define subvarieties <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}(E,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}(E,F)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}(E)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{C}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{C}(F)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> analogously.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Abusing notation, we denote the images of these loci in the unframed versions <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> by the same letters. The framed versus unframed setting is usually clear by context, and sometimes irrelevant, for example in discussing the codimensions. We caution the reader that these loci are not necessarily irreducible or of expected dimension (<a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="4.3. ‣ 4 Degree four ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="4.4. ‣ 4 Degree four ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>). Even determining whether they are non-empty remains a challenge in full generality.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>The associated scroll construction</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze the Maroni loci <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(E)</annotation></semantics></math>, we associate to a cover of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> a curve on a Hirzebruch surface. The construction is originally due to Ohbuchi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ohb97</a>]</cite>. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be an irreducible curve of arithmetic genus <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> a finite cover of degree <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> be a global section of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{C}(m)=\alpha^{*}O_{{\bf P}^{1}}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{C}(m)=\alpha^{*}O_{{\bf P}^{1}}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> that projects to a nonzero section of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\vee}_{\alpha}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\vee}_{\alpha}(m)</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> is not a pullback of a section from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The section <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> gives a map from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to the total space of the line bundle <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(m)</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{F}}_{m}=\operatorname{Proj}(O\oplus O(-m))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Proj</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{F}}_{m}=\operatorname{Proj}(O\oplus O(-m))</annotation></semantics></math> be the Hirzebruch surface that compactifies this total space. We thus get the diagram</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

