<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\subset{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\subset{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be the directrix and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\subset{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\subset{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> the section disjoint from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (so that <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}=-m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}=-m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{2}=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{2}=m</annotation></semantics></math>). By construction, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(C)\subset{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(C)\subset{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> avoids the directrix <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is smooth and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> does not factor nontrivially. Then <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> is birational onto its image, and therefore <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(C)</annotation></semantics></math> is a reduced and irreducible element of the linear system <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d\tau|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d\tau|</annotation></semantics></math>. By the following proposition, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(C)</annotation></semantics></math> is a point in the Severi variety <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A reduced and irreducible curve on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> of geometric genus <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> in the linear system <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d\tau|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d\tau|</annotation></semantics></math> is a flat limit of irreducible nodal curves of geometric genus <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{C}\subset{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{C}\subset{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be such a reduced and irreducible curve. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to\overline{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to\overline{C}</annotation></semantics></math> the normalization. Denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> the composite map <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu{\colon}C\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu{\colon}C\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> be a component of the Kontsevich space of maps <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> be the normal sheaf of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>; this is the cokernel of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{C}\to\nu^{*}T_{{\mathbf{F}}_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{C}\to\nu^{*}T_{{\mathbf{F}}_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we have a lower bound: <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\mathcal{M}\geq\chi(N_{\nu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\mathcal{M}\geq\chi(N_{\nu})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(N_{\nu})=\chi(\nu^{*}T_{{\mathbf{F}}_{m}})-\chi(T_{C})=g-\deg(K_{{\mathbf{F}}_{m}}\cdot\overline{C})-1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(N_{\nu})=\chi(\nu^{*}T_{{\mathbf{F}}_{m}})-\chi(T_{C})=g-\deg(K_{{\mathbf{F}}_{m}}\cdot\overline{C})-1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">we get</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\mathcal{M}\geq g-\deg(K_{{\mathbf{F}}_{m}}\cdot\overline{C})-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\mathcal{M}\geq g-\deg(K_{{\mathbf{F}}_{m}}\cdot\overline{C})-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har86</a>, Proposition 2.2]</cite>, a general <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{\rm gen}{\colon}C_{\rm gen}\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mi>gen</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>gen</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{\rm gen}{\colon}C_{\rm gen}\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is birational onto its image and the image has only nodes as singularities.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We can make the construction in a family. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a reduced scheme, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho{\colon}C\to M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho{\colon}C\to M</annotation></semantics></math> a generically smooth family of reduced and irreducible curves of genus <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}\times M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}\times M</annotation></semantics></math> a finite flat <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>-morphism of degree <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{C}(m)=\alpha^{*}O(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{C}(m)=\alpha^{*}O(m)</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that none of the fibers <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}{\colon}C_{t}\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}{\colon}C_{t}\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> factor nontrivially and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(C_{t},O_{C_{t}}(m))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(C_{t},O_{C_{t}}(m))</annotation></semantics></math> has constant rank. Then <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{*}O_{C}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{*}O_{C}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector bundle on <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. The trivial subbundle <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}({\bf P}^{1},O(m))\otimes O_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}({\bf P}^{1},O(m))\otimes O_{M}</annotation></semantics></math> maps injectively to <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{*}O_{C}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{*}O_{C}(m)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be the complement of the image of this map in the total space of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{*}O_{C}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{*}O_{C}(m)</annotation></semantics></math>. Fiberwise, the sections of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to the sections <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> which project nontrivially onto <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}^{\vee}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}^{\vee}(m)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the associated scroll construction gives a morphism</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\to\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\to\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">We will use this construction where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a Maroni locus. As described, the construction depends on the existence of a universal family, and thus gives a morphism from the fine moduli stack. But since <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> is a scheme, we get a canonical induced map from the coarse space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The following crucial result makes the above construction useful.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.3</span></span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tyo07</a><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">]</span></cite><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All Severi varieties parametrizing irreducible curves on Hirzebruch surfaces are irreducible and of expected dimension. In particular, the variety <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible of dimension <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dm+2d+g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dm+2d+g-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We also need the following result, which we prove for the lack of a reference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{C}\subset{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{C}\subset{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be a general point of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to\overline{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to\overline{C}</annotation></semantics></math> the normalization. Then the composite <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is simply branched.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">In light of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="2.3[]. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem 2.3</span></a>, it suffices to exhibit a particular <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{C}</annotation></semantics></math> of geometric genus <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> whose normalization is simply branched over <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. One way is to start with <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X={\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X={\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}X\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}X\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> a simply branched cover of degree <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}=O(1)^{\oplus(d-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}=O(1)^{\oplus(d-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Choosing a general section of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\vee}_{\alpha}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\vee}_{\alpha}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> gives <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu{\colon}X\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu{\colon}X\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is nodal. It is easy to see that <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is in the closure of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, since the set of nodes of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(X)</annotation></semantics></math> impose independent conditions on <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|K_{{\mathbf{F}}_{m}}+d\tau|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|K_{{\mathbf{F}}_{m}}+d\tau|</annotation></semantics></math>, they automatically impose independent conditions on <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d\tau|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d\tau|</annotation></semantics></math> as well, and hence we may smooth out the required number of nodes of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(X)</annotation></semantics></math> to deform to a curve of geometric genus <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. A general fiber of such a smoothing is the required <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can realize the associated scroll construction geometrically as follows. The choice of a general global section <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{C}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{C}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> can be thought of as a choice of a geometric section <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma{\colon}{\bf P}^{1}\to{\bf P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma{\colon}{\bf P}^{1}\to{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math>. In the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{d-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{d-2}</annotation></semantics></math> fibers of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi{\colon}{\bf P}E\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi{\colon}{\bf P}E\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we now have <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d+1</annotation></semantics></math> points: <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> points coming from the fibers of the map <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and one more point provided by the section <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. For general <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, these <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d+1</annotation></semantics></math> points will be in general position, and so will define a unique rational normal curve <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}\subset{\bf P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}\subset{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the birationally ruled surface <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset{\bf P}E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset{\bf P}E</annotation></semantics></math> defined as the closure of the union of the <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>’s. <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> contains both <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, and is fibered over <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We contract all components of the fibers of the projection <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi{\colon}S\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi{\colon}S\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> which do not meet the directrix <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>. The resulting surface is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> being the directrix. The image of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> under the contraction <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is the associated scroll construction.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">For a vector bundle <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=O(a_{1})\oplus\dots\oplus O(a_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=O(a_{1})\oplus\dots\oplus O(a_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, set</p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor E\rfloor=\min\{a_{i}\}\text{ and }\lceil E\rceil=\max\{a_{i}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor E\rfloor=\min\{a_{i}\}\text{ and }\lceil E\rceil=\max\{a_{i}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Given a cover <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the associated scroll construction <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu{\colon}C\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu{\colon}C\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> can be made for <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq\lfloor E_{\alpha}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq\lfloor E_{\alpha}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. Conversely, given a point <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{C}\in\mathcal{V}_{g}^{\rm irr}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>irr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{C}\in\mathcal{V}_{g}^{\rm irr}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to\overline{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to\overline{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be the normalization. Then the induced cover <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor E_{\alpha}\rfloor\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor E_{\alpha}\rfloor\leq m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty, then</span></p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil E\rceil\leq\frac{2g+2d-2}{d}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil E\rceil\leq\frac{2g+2d-2}{d}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore, if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> comes from a cover <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> irreducible, and where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> does not factor nontrivially, then</span></p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{g+d-1}{{d\choose 2}}\leq\lfloor E_{\alpha}\rfloor\leq\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{g+d-1}{{d\choose 2}}\leq\lfloor E_{\alpha}\rfloor\leq\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">The resolution of <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="1.4. ‣ 1.2 The Casnati–Ekedahl structure theorem ‣ 1 Preliminaries ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem 1.4</span></a> tells us that <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset{\bf P}E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset{\bf P}E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is not contained in any hyperplane divisor. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> denote the hyperplane divisor class associated to <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{{\bf P}E_{\alpha}}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{{\bf P}E_{\alpha}}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> denote the class of the fiber of <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi{\colon}{\bf P}E\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi{\colon}{\bf P}E\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N:=\lceil E_{\alpha}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N:=\lceil E_{\alpha}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the divisor class <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h-Nf" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h-Nf</annotation></semantics></math> is effective. Since <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible and does not lie in <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(h-Nf)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(h-Nf)</annotation></semantics></math>, it intersects <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(h-Nf)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(h-Nf)</annotation></semantics></math> non-negatively. Since <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\cdot[C]=2g+2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\cdot[C]=2g+2d-2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\cdot[C]=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\cdot[C]=d</annotation></semantics></math>, we conclude that <math id="S2.SS1.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\leq\frac{2d+2g-2}{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\leq\frac{2d+2g-2}{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the second inequality, we appeal to the associated scroll construction. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n:=\lfloor E_{\alpha}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n:=\lfloor E_{\alpha}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> does not factor, <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu{\colon}C\to{\mathbf{F}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu{\colon}C\to{\mathbf{F}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> must be birational onto its image. Adjunction on <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{F}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{F}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> gives</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a}(\nu(C))={d\choose 2}n-(d-1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a}(\nu(C))={d\choose 2}n-(d-1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">The second statement now follows from the inequality <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq p_{a}(\nu(C))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq p_{a}(\nu(C))</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The following theorem of Ohbuchi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ohb97</a>]</cite> places a strong restriction on a large class of Tschirnhausen bundles <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.7</span></span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ohb97</a><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">]</span></cite><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a cover of degree <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> irreducible, and where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> does not factor nontrivially. Write <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}=O(a_{1})\oplus\dots\oplus O(a_{d-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}=O(a_{1})\oplus\dots\oplus O(a_{d-1})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor E_{\alpha}\rfloor=a_{1}\leq a_{2}\leq\dots\leq a_{d-1}=\lceil E_{\alpha}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor E_{\alpha}\rfloor=a_{1}\leq a_{2}\leq\dots\leq a_{d-1}=\lceil E_{\alpha}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then</span></p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i+1}-a_{i}\leq\lfloor E_{\alpha}\rfloor\quad\text{for $1\leq i\leq d-2$}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">for </mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i+1}-a_{i}\leq\lfloor E_{\alpha}\rfloor\quad\text{for $1\leq i\leq d-2$}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="2.7[]. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>implies the second inequality in <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="2.6. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We call a vector bundle <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d-1</annotation></semantics></math> and degree <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+d-1</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tame</em> if it satisfies the inequalities (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2.6. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>), (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.6. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>), and (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.7[]. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="2.6. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="2.7[]. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> imply that <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> is tame in the following two cases: <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is simply branched, or <math id="S2.SS1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is prime. Indeed, in either case, the cover cannot factor non-trivially.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leadsto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↝</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leadsto</annotation></semantics></math> the partial order on vector bundles on <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> given by <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\leadsto E^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">↝</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\leadsto E^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> specializes to <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in a flat family. Define the finite set <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}[m]</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{T}}[m]:=\{\text{Isomorphism classes of tame bundles $E$ of rank $d-1$, degree $g+d-1$, and $\lfloor E\rfloor=m$}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>Isomorphism classes of tame bundles </mtext><mi>E</mi><mtext> of rank </mtext><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mtext>, degree </mtext><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mtext>, and </mtext><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{T}}[m]:=\{\text{Isomorphism classes of tame bundles $E$ of rank $d-1$, degree $g+d-1$, and $\lfloor E\rfloor=m$}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p12.2" class="ltx_p">Observe that <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}[m]</annotation></semantics></math> contains an element <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[m]</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[m]\leadsto E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↝</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[m]\leadsto E</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\in\mathcal{T}[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\in\mathcal{T}[m]</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[m]</annotation></semantics></math> is the most generic among all the bundles in <math id="S2.SS1.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}[m]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer satisfying <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{g+d-1}{{d\choose 2}}\leq m\leq\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{g+d-1}{{d\choose 2}}\leq m\leq\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(E)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is nonempty, then </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a tame bundle.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor E\rfloor\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor E\rfloor\leq m</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> then </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(E)\subset M(E[m])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(E)\subset M(E[m])</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(E[m])\subset M(E[m+1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(E[m])\subset M(E[m+1])</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for all </span><math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(E[m])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(E[m])</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is an irreducible subvariety of </span><math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of codimension </span><math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-(d-1)m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-(d-1)m+1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> unless </span><math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\lfloor\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\lfloor\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, in which case </span><math id="S2.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(E[m])=\mathcal{H}_{d,g}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(E[m])=\mathcal{H}_{d,g}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is prime, then all the statements above hold with </span><math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(-)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(-)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> replaced by </span><math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}(-)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}(-)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\dagger}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\dagger}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> replaced by </span><math id="S2.I1.i5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}^{\dagger}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}^{\dagger}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">In the proof, we use a theorem of Coppens, which we state using our setup.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.11</span></span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cop99</a><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">]</span></cite><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{g+d-1}{{d\choose 2}}\leq m\leq\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{g+d-1}{{d\choose 2}}\leq m\leq\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a genus <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and degree <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> cover <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with Tschirnhausen bundle <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[m]</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is birational onto its image under the associated scroll construction <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="2.10. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem 2.10</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">We repeatedly use simultaneous normalization in the following way: Suppose we have a family <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}\to\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}\to\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> of reduced irreducible curves of geometric genus <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the normalization <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> gives a family <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{\nu}\to\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>ν</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{\nu}\to\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> of smooth curves of genus <math id="S2.SS1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tei80</a>]</cite>. For the ease of reading, we do not make this process explicit every time.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">The first statement follows from <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="2.6. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="2.7[]. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the second statement, first note that if <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\overline{C}]\in\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\overline{C}]\in\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> is a general point and <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu{\colon}C\to\overline{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu{\colon}C\to\overline{C}</annotation></semantics></math> the normalization, then <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is simply branched and has Tschirnhausen bundle <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[m]</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, we can get a <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\overline{C}]\in\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\overline{C}]\in\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> with Tschirnhausen module <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[m]</annotation></semantics></math> by applying the associated scroll construction to a cover given by <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem11" title="2.11[]. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem 2.11</span></a>. By <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="2.4. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, we may deform such <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\overline{C}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\overline{C}]</annotation></semantics></math> so that the normalization is simply branched. By the genericity of <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[m]</annotation></semantics></math>, the normalization of the deformed curve also has Tschirnhausen bundle <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[m]</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, suppose <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor E\rfloor\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor E\rfloor\leq m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[C\to{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[C\to{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> is a point with Tschirnhausen bundle <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the associated scroll construction gives <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu{\colon}C\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu{\colon}C\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is simply branched, <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> is birational onto its image. Then <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(C)</annotation></semantics></math> is the limit of curves in <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> whose normalization has Tschirnhausen bundle <math id="S2.SS1.p16.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[m]</annotation></semantics></math>. The second statement follows.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">The third statement is a corollary of the second statement.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the fourth statement, suppose <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\lfloor\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\lfloor\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[m]</annotation></semantics></math> is balanced, so <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(E[m])=\mathcal{H}_{d,g}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(E[m])=\mathcal{H}_{d,g}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&lt;\lfloor\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&lt;\lfloor\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> be the locus of nodal curves of geometric genus <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> whose normalization is simply branched over <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is a smooth open subset of <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>. Normalization of the universal family of curves in <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> of geometric genus <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> gives a family of smooth curves of genus <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> with a simply branched map of degree <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (induced from <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{F}}_{m}\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{F}}_{m}\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.) By definition, the image is in <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(E[m])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(E[m])</annotation></semantics></math>. We thus get a dominant map</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q{\colon}U\to M(E[m])." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q{\colon}U\to M(E[m]).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p18.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p18.2.1" class="ltx_text">The fiber of <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the global sections of <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{C}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{C}(m)</annotation></semantics></math> that project non-trivially onto <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\vee}(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\vee}(m)</annotation></semantics></math>. For general <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in M(E[m])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in M(E[m])</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{\alpha}=E[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{\alpha}=E[m]</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, since <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&lt;\lfloor\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&lt;\lfloor\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>, the bundle <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[m]</annotation></semantics></math> has a unique <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(m)</annotation></semantics></math> summand and all other summands have degree greater than <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the general fiber of <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> has dimension <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+2</annotation></semantics></math>. From the dimension of <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>, we get</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim M(E[m])=\dim\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)-(m+2)=(d-1)m+g+2d-3." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim M(E[m])=\dim\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)-(m+2)=(d-1)m+g+2d-3.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p18.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p18.3.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S2.SS1.p18.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\mathcal{H}^{\dagger}_{d,g}=2g+2d-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\mathcal{H}^{\dagger}_{d,g}=2g+2d-2</annotation></semantics></math>, the fourth statement follows.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the last statement, note that all the arguments hold for <math id="S2.SS1.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{M}(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{M}(E)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS1.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is prime, since the associated scroll construction <math id="S2.SS1.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu{\colon}C\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu{\colon}C\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is automatically birational onto its image.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="2.10. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem 2.10</span></a>gives us good control on the dimensions of the Maroni loci for <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> based on the minimal summand of <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>. We must now consider those <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> which are non-generic, but nonetheless have the same minimal summand as the generic Tschirnhausen bundle. Set <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\lfloor\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\lfloor\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</p>
<table id="S2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[k]=O(k)^{\oplus r}\oplus O(k+1)^{\oplus d-r-1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[k]=O(k)^{\oplus r}\oplus O(k+1)^{\oplus d-r-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p20.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;r\leq d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;r\leq d-1</annotation></semantics></math>. A general cover <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathcal{H}^{\dagger}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathcal{H}^{\dagger}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E[k]</annotation></semantics></math> as its Tschirnhausen bundle. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be any tame bundle, and set <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s:=h^{0}({E^{\prime}}^{\vee}(k))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>E</mi><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo><mrow></mrow><mo>∨</mo></mmultiscripts><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s:=h^{0}({E^{\prime}}^{\vee}(k))</annotation></semantics></math>. Upper semicontinuity implies <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\geq r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\geq r</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&gt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&gt;r</annotation></semantics></math>. Define</p>
<table id="S2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\circ}(E^{\prime})=\left\{\alpha\in\mathcal{H}^{\dagger}_{d,g}\mid E_{\alpha}\cong E^{\prime}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\circ}(E^{\prime})=\left\{\alpha\in\mathcal{H}^{\dagger}_{d,g}\mid E_{\alpha}\cong E^{\prime}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p20.3" class="ltx_p">Then <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\circ}(E^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\circ}(E^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is locally closed, and <math id="S2.SS1.p20.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{M^{\circ}(E^{\prime})}=M(E^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{M^{\circ}(E^{\prime})}=M(E^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.12</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under the assumptions above, let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset M^{\circ}(E^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>∘</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset M^{\circ}(E^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> be any irreducible component. Then the codimension of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}^{\dagger}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}^{\dagger}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s-r)+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s-r)+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=\dim Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=\dim Z</annotation></semantics></math>. We use the associated scroll construction over <math id="S2.SS1.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>. We have an open subset <math id="S2.SS1.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> of a vector bundle of rank <math id="S2.SS1.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s+k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s+k+1</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.SS1.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> and a morphism <math id="S2.SS1.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\to\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{k},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\to\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{k},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.SS1.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E^{\prime}\neq E[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E^{\prime}\neq E[k]</annotation></semantics></math>, the closure of the image of <math id="S2.SS1.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is a proper subvariety of <math id="S2.SS1.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{k},\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{k},\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, we have <math id="S2.SS1.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim U&lt;\dim\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{k},d\tau)=dk+2d+g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim U&lt;\dim\mathcal{V}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{k},d\tau)=dk+2d+g-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The lemma follows from this inequality.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p22.1" class="ltx_p">We now have the tools to determine all the Maroni divisors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.13</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Maroni locus <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(E)\subset\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(E)\subset\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a divisor if and only if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=(k-1)(d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=(k-1)(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math> for some integer <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E=E[k-1]=O(k-1)\oplus O(k)^{\oplus d-3}\oplus O(k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E=E[k-1]=O(k-1)\oplus O(k)^{\oplus d-3}\oplus O(k+1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, in this situation, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(E[k-1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(E[k-1])</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.SS1.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor E\rfloor=k=\lfloor\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor E\rfloor=k=\lfloor\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>, then the statement follows by applying <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem12" title="2.12. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.12</span></a>. If, on the other hand, <math id="S2.SS1.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor E\rfloor&lt;\lfloor\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor E\rfloor&lt;\lfloor\frac{g+d-1}{d-1}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>, then the statement follows from statement <math id="S2.SS1.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="2.10. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem 2.10</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p24.1" class="ltx_p">We record a particularly interesting case of the irreducibility of the Maroni divisor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem14" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem14.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.14</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2(d-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2(d-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(E[2])\subset\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(E[2])\subset\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is irreducible, and it is the ramification locus of the generically finite and dominant forgetful map <math id="S2.Thmtheorem14.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu{\colon}\mathcal{H}_{d,g}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu{\colon}\mathcal{H}_{d,g}\to\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.8" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text">The irreducibility statement follows from <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="2.10. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem 2.10</span></a>. To show that <math id="S2.SS1.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(E[2])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(E[2])</annotation></semantics></math> is the ramification locus of <math id="S2.SS1.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, consider <math id="S2.SS1.p25.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\in\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\in\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> and the map of sheaves:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\alpha^{*}(T_{{\bf P}^{1}})\to T_{C}\to N_{\alpha}\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\alpha^{*}(T_{{\bf P}^{1}})\to T_{C}\to N_{\alpha}\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p25.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p25.2.1" class="ltx_text">The tangent space to <math id="S2.SS1.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.SS1.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS1.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(C,N_{\alpha})/\alpha^{*}H^{0}({\bf P}^{1},T_{{\bf P}^{1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(C,N_{\alpha})/\alpha^{*}H^{0}({\bf P}^{1},T_{{\bf P}^{1}})</annotation></semantics></math> and the tangent space to <math id="S2.SS1.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> at <math id="S2.SS1.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS1.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(C,T_{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(C,T_{C})</annotation></semantics></math>. The map</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\mu{\colon}H^{0}(C,N_{\alpha})/\alpha^{*}H^{0}({\bf P}^{1},T_{{\bf P}^{1}})\to H^{1}(C,T_{C})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\mu{\colon}H^{0}(C,N_{\alpha})/\alpha^{*}H^{0}({\bf P}^{1},T_{{\bf P}^{1}})\to H^{1}(C,T_{C})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p25.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p25.3.1" class="ltx_text">fails to be surjective precisely when <math id="S2.SS1.p25.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(C,\alpha^{*}T_{{\bf P}^{1}})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(C,\alpha^{*}T_{{\bf P}^{1}})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, when <math id="S2.SS1.p25.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in M(E[2])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in M(E[2])</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Linear independence of <math id="S2.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we prove that the divisorial components of the boundary of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly independent. Define the closed loci <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex18.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\overline{\{[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\mid\alpha^{-1}(q)=3p_{1}+p_{2}+\dots+p_{d-2}\text{ for some $q$ and distinct $p_{i}$.}\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐏</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>α</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext> for some </mtext><mi>q</mi><mtext> and distinct </mtext><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\overline{\{[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\mid\alpha^{-1}(q)=3p_{1}+p_{2}+\dots+p_{d-2}\text{ for some $q$ and distinct $p_{i}$.}\}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex19.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\overline{\{[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\mid\alpha^{-1}(q)=2p_{1}+2p_{2}+p_{3}+\dots+p_{d-2}\text{ for some $q$ and distinct $p_{i}$.}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐏</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>α</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext> for some </mtext><mi>q</mi><mtext> and distinct </mtext><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\overline{\{[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\mid\alpha^{-1}(q)=2p_{1}+2p_{2}+p_{3}+\dots+p_{d-2}\text{ for some $q$ and distinct $p_{i}$.}}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex20.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\overline{\{[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\mid C\text{ is singular.}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝐏</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>C</mi><mtext> is singular.</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\overline{\{[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]\mid C\text{ is singular.}}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">These three loci correspond to the three possibilities of the limit when two branch points of a branched cover come together. Note that <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> are irreducible and their union is the complement of <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem15" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.15</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, the classes of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> are linearly independent in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}(\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}(\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g})</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, the same is true for the classes of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem15.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We construct curves with non-singular intersection matrix with our divisors. For this, a slight enlargement of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is more convenient. Define <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}^{ns}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}^{ns}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> as the moduli space of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is an at worst nodal curve of arithmetic genus <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, not necessarily irreducible, but without any separating nodes, and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is a map of degree <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. The target <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is taken to be unframed. It is easy to see that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a dense open subset of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{ns}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{ns}</annotation></semantics></math> with codimension two complement. Abusing notation, we denote the closures of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{ns}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{ns}</annotation></semantics></math> by the same letters. It suffices to prove the proposition for <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{ns}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{ns}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now construct test curves in <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{ns}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{ns}</annotation></semantics></math>. Pick non-negative integers <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}+g_{2}=g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}+g_{2}=g-1</annotation></semantics></math> and positive integers <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}+d_{2}=d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}+d_{2}=d</annotation></semantics></math>. Take a family <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{b}{\colon}X_{b}\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{b}{\colon}X_{b}\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of covers of degree <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and genus <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a parameter on a smooth complete curve <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that we have two sections <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p,q{\colon}B\to X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p,q{\colon}B\to X</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{b}(p_{b})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{b}(p_{b})=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{b}(q_{b})=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{b}(q_{b})=\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in B</annotation></semantics></math>. Take <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta{\colon}E\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta{\colon}E\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to be a fixed simply branched cover of degree <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and genus <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, unramified over <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\prime},q^{\prime}\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\prime},q^{\prime}\in E</annotation></semantics></math> be two points over <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\infty</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. Our test curve in <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{ns}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{ns}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the family <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{b}{\colon}C_{b}\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{b}{\colon}C_{b}\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by gluing <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{b},p_{b},q_{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{b},p_{b},q_{b})</annotation></semantics></math> to the constant family <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(E,p^{\prime},q^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(E,p^{\prime},q^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{b}{\colon}C_{b}\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{b}{\colon}C_{b}\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is induced from <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{\colon}X_{b}\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{\colon}X_{b}\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta{\colon}E\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta{\colon}E\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The construction is depicted in <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2.2 Linear independence of 
          
            T
          
        , 
          
            D
          
        , and 
          
            Δ ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.F1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="184.05" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 289.53 184.05" width="289.53"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,184.05) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(118.39,0) translate(0,136.53)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -118.11 0 C -118.11 0 -36.07 8.76 -19.69 19.69 C -3.3 30.61 -64.52 31.72 -59.06 39.37 C -53.59 47.02 -32.78 47.24 0 47.24 C 32.78 47.24 53.59 47.02 59.06 39.37 C 64.52 31.72 3.3 30.61 19.69 19.69 C 36.07 8.76 118.11 0 118.11 0"></path><path d="M -78.74 4.72 M -76.77 4.72 C -76.77 5.81 -77.65 6.69 -78.74 6.69 C -79.83 6.69 -80.71 5.81 -80.71 4.72 C -80.71 3.64 -79.83 2.76 -78.74 2.76 C -77.65 2.76 -76.77 3.64 -76.77 4.72 Z M -78.74 4.72"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -85 12.3)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.9em;--ltx-fo-height:0.43em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.65" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.96)" width="12.52"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.F1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{b}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path d="M 78.74 4.72 M 80.71 4.72 C 80.71 5.81 79.83 6.69 78.74 6.69 C 77.65 6.69 76.77 5.81 76.77 4.72 C 76.77 3.64 77.65 2.76 78.74 2.76 C 79.83 2.76 80.71 3.64 80.71 4.72 Z M 78.74 4.72"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 72.62 12.3)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.88em;--ltx-fo-height:0.43em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.65" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 5.96)" width="12.23"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.F1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{b}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 157.48 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 148.43 -3.69)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.31em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="18.11"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.F1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{b}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M -118.11 -27.56 C -118.11 -27.56 118.11 -13.15 118.11 -39.37 C 118.11 -65.59 -118.11 -51.18 -118.11 -51.18"></path><path d="M -78.74 -26.38 M -76.77 -26.38 C -76.77 -25.29 -77.65 -24.41 -78.74 -24.41 C -79.83 -24.41 -80.71 -25.29 -80.71 -26.38 C -80.71 -27.47 -79.83 -28.35 -78.74 -28.35 C -77.65 -28.35 -76.77 -27.47 -76.77 -26.38 Z M -78.74 -26.38"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -84.16 -41.67)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.09" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.4)" width="10.84"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.F1.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\pr