<p id="S6.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Define five tautological divisor classes on <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> (The subscript <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> stands for “Severi”):</p>
<ol id="S6.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{s}:=c_{1}(\rho_{*}\omega_{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{s}:=c_{1}(\rho_{*}\omega_{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{s}:=\rho_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{\rho})^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{s}:=\rho_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{\rho})^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{s}:=\rho_{*}(\nu^{*}(f)\cdot c_{1}(\omega_{\rho}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{s}:=\rho_{*}(\nu^{*}(f)\cdot c_{1}(\omega_{\rho}))</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{s}:=\rho_{*}(\nu^{*}(\sigma)\cdot c_{1}(\omega_{\rho}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{s}:=\rho_{*}(\nu^{*}(\sigma)\cdot c_{1}(\omega_{\rho}))</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{s}:=\rho_{*}(\nu^{*}\text{[Point]})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ν</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>[Point]</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{s}:=\rho_{*}(\nu^{*}\text{[Point]})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S6.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Since the irreducible curves in the linear system <math id="S6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|d\tau|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|d\tau|</annotation></semantics></math> avoid the directrix <math id="S6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, we get <math id="S6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{s}=\psi_{s}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{s}=\psi_{s}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, a natural conjecture is the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The rational Picard group of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological, that is</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{{\bf Q}}\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}={\bf Q}\langle\lambda_{s},\kappa_{s},\xi_{s}\rangle." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{{\bf Q}}\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}={\bf Q}\langle\lambda_{s},\kappa_{s},\xi_{s}\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Denote by <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CU" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CU</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TN" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TN</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TP</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> the closures in <math id="S6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\rm irr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>irr</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\rm irr}</annotation></semantics></math> of the locus curves with a cusp, tacnode, triple point, or an additional node, respectively. Abusing notation, denote their preimages in <math id="S6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> by the same letters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is not hard to check that the classes in <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CU" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CU</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TN" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TN</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TP</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear combinations of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and vice versa. <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="6.1. ‣ 6 From Hurwitz spaces to Severi varieties ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> is therefore equivalent to</p>
<table id="S6.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\mathcal{U}=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\mathcal{U}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6.3</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The only divisorial components of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\rm irr}\setminus\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>irr</mi></msup><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\rm irr}\setminus\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="CU" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">CU</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TN" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TN</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TP</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">It suffices to show that the codimension one components of <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}\setminus\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}\setminus\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math> are the loci of curves with cusps, tacnodes, triple points or an additional node. This follows by the same proof as for Theorem 1.4 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">DH88b</a>]</cite>. The critical ingredient of the argument is provided by <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem4" title="6.4. ‣ 6 From Hurwitz spaces to Severi varieties ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6.4</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in|d\tau|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in|d\tau|</annotation></semantics></math> be a reduced irreducible curve on the Hirzebruch surface <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> the conductor ideal of the singularities of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> imposes independent conditions on <math id="S6.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},O(d\tau))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},O(d\tau))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=K_{{\mathbf{F}}_{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=K_{{\mathbf{F}}_{m}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the canonical class. The anti-canonical class <math id="S6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-K</annotation></semantics></math> is effective. Furthermore, the fixed component of <math id="S6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-K</annotation></semantics></math> is the directrix <math id="S6.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-K</annotation></semantics></math> separates points away from <math id="S6.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">It is a classical that <math id="S6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> imposes independent conditions on the adjoint linear system <math id="S6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|K+D|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|K+D|</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=V(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=V(A)</annotation></semantics></math> be the zero dimensional scheme defined by the ideal sheaf <math id="S6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the restriction map</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(O(K+D))\to H^{0}(O_{Z}(K+D))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(O(K+D))\to H^{0}(O_{Z}(K+D))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">is surjective. Therefore, we can conclude the same for</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(O(D))\to H^{0}(O_{Z}(D))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>Z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(O(D))\to H^{0}(O_{Z}(D))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">by multiplying the previous restriction map by a general section of <math id="S6.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(-K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(-K)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We now rephrase the Picard rank conjecture for Hurwitz spaces in a manner similar to <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="6.1. ‣ 6 From Hurwitz spaces to Severi varieties ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>. Consider the diagram</p>
<table id="S6.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox to65.02pt{\vbox to62.86pt{\pgfpicture\makeatletter\hbox{\hskip 12.83827pt\lower-51.68768pt\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke} \lxSVG@begingroup@{fill} \lxSVG@setlinewidth{\the\pgflinewidth}\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nullfont\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin} 
{{}}\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\hbox{\hbox{{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin} {{}{}{{
{}{}}}{
{}{}}
{{}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}}{{}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{}{{}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}
{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces
}{{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin} \lxSVG@transformcm{1.0}{0.0}{0.0}{1.0}{-3.61111pt}{-3.41666pt}\lxSVG@begingroup@{transform} \pgfsys@hbox{60}\lxSVG@closescope }}}
\lxSVG@closescope }}}
{{{
{}{}{}}}}{{}}\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\hbox{\hbox{{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin} {{}{}{{
{}{}}}{
{}{}}
{{}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}}{{}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{}{{}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}
