<p id="S6.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Define the following tautological divisor classes on <math id="S6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> (The subscript “h” stands for “Hurwitz”):</p>
<ol id="S6.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S6.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{h}:=c_{1}(f_{*}\omega_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{h}:=c_{1}(f_{*}\omega_{f})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{h}:=f_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{f})^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{h}:=f_{*}(c_{1}(\omega_{f})^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{h}:=f_{*}(\alpha^{*}\text{[Point]}\cdot c_{1}(\omega_{f}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>[Point]</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{h}:=f_{*}(\alpha^{*}\text{[Point]}\cdot c_{1}(\omega_{f}))</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6.5</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The rational Picard group of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}^{\dagger}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}^{\dagger}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is tautological, that is,
<math id="S6.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{{\bf Q}}\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}^{\dagger}_{d,g}={\bf Q}\langle\lambda_{h},\kappa_{h},\xi_{h}\rangle." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{{\bf Q}}\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}^{\dagger}_{d,g}={\bf Q}\langle\lambda_{h},\kappa_{h},\xi_{h}\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">6.6</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is easy to see that the classes of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> can be
expressed as <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear combinations of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S6.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> and vice versa. Also, by <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="1.3. ‣ 1.1 Picard groups of quotients ‣ 1 Preliminaries ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>,
the framed/unframed distinction is irrelevant. Therefore,
<a href="#S6.Thmtheorem5" title="6.5. ‣ 6 From Hurwitz spaces to Severi varieties ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a> is equivalent to the Picard rank conjecture
stated in the introduction, namely that</p>
<table id="S6.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\mathcal{H}_{d,g}=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\mathcal{H}_{d,g}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We now state the main theorem of this section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6.7</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq\lfloor(g+d-1)/(d-1)\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq\lfloor(g+d-1)/(d-1)\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>, then <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="6.1. ‣ 6 From Hurwitz spaces to Severi varieties ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> implies <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem5" title="6.5. ‣ 6 From Hurwitz spaces to Severi varieties ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a> for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq\lceil 2(g+d-1)/d\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq\lceil 2(g+d-1)/d\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>, then <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="6.1. ‣ 6 From Hurwitz spaces to Severi varieties ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem5" title="6.5. ‣ 6 From Hurwitz spaces to Severi varieties ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a> for <math id="S6.Thmtheorem7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S6.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq\lfloor(g+d-1)/(d-1)\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq\lfloor(g+d-1)/(d-1)\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. Retain the notation introduced in this section. In particular, abbreviate <math id="S6.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{g}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},d\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S6.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so on. Let <math id="S6.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi{\colon}{\mathbf{F}}_{m}\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi{\colon}{\mathbf{F}}_{m}\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the projection and <math id="S6.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\subset{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\subset{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> the directrix. Fix a section <math id="S6.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta\in H^{0}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},\pi^{*}O(m))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta\in H^{0}({\mathbf{F}}_{m},\pi^{*}O(m))</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to a smooth element of the linear series <math id="S6.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\tau|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\tau|</annotation></semantics></math>. We view <math id="S6.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{F}}_{m}\setminus\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{F}}_{m}\setminus\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> as the total space of the line bundle <math id="S6.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(m)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> as the tautological section of <math id="S6.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}O(m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}O(m)</annotation></semantics></math> on this total space.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S6.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi{\colon}\mathcal{C}\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi{\colon}\mathcal{C}\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the composite <math id="S6.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi=\pi\circ\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi=\pi\circ\nu</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\subset\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\subset\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the open subset consisting of <math id="S6.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(\mathcal{C}_{u},\phi^{*}O(m))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(\mathcal{C}_{u},\phi^{*}O(m))</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal. Likewise, let <math id="S6.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\subset\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\subset\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subset consisting of <math id="S6.p11.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(C,\alpha^{*}O(m))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(C,\alpha^{*}O(m))</annotation></semantics></math> is minimal. By <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem13" title="2.13. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.13</span></a>, the complement of <math id="S6.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}_{d,g}^{\dagger}</annotation></semantics></math> has codimension at least two. Let <math id="S6.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> be the total space of the vector bundle <math id="S6.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{*}\alpha^{*}O(m)|_{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>W</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{*}\alpha^{*}O(m)|_{W}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S6.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have a birational morphism <math id="S6.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q{\colon}Z\to V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q{\colon}Z\to V</annotation></semantics></math> defined as follows. A point <math id="S6.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped to <math id="S6.p12.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\phi_{u}{\colon}\mathcal{C}_{u}\to{\bf P}^{1},v]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\phi_{u}{\colon}\mathcal{C}_{u}\to{\bf P}^{1},v]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in H^{0}(\mathcal{C}_{u},\phi_{u}^{*}O(m))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in H^{0}(\mathcal{C}_{u},\phi_{u}^{*}O(m))</annotation></semantics></math> is the restriction of <math id="S6.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math>. To define the inverse, we must restrict to an open subset of <math id="S6.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S6.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset V</annotation></semantics></math> be the open subset consisting of <math id="S6.p12.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="([\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}],v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">([\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}],v)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in H^{0}(C,\alpha^{*}O(m))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in H^{0}(C,\alpha^{*}O(m))</annotation></semantics></math> is such that the lift of <math id="S6.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to{\bf P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to{\bf P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by <math id="S6.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is birational onto its image. We then get a morphism <math id="S6.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{\colon}X\to\mathcal{V}^{\rm irr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>irr</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{\colon}X\to\mathcal{V}^{\rm irr}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is quasi-finite, and generically one-to-one. Let <math id="S6.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\subset X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\subset X</annotation></semantics></math> be the open subset consisting of points whose associated element in <math id="S6.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}^{\rm irr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mi>irr</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}^{\rm irr}</annotation></semantics></math> has at worst a cusp, a tacnode, a triple point, or an additional node. By <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem3" title="6.3. ‣ 6 From Hurwitz spaces to Severi varieties ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>, and the quasi-finiteness of <math id="S6.p12.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, the complement of <math id="S6.p12.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p12.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> has codimension at least two. Since <math id="S6.p12.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is normal, we get a morphism <math id="S6.p12.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{\colon}Y\to Z\subset\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{\colon}Y\to Z\subset\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>, inverse to <math id="S6.p12.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. We summarize the spaces we have defined and their relationships in the following diagram.</span></p>
