<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">We may thus drop the subscripts and use <math id="S6.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the corresponding divisors on any of the spaces in (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 6 From Hurwitz spaces to Severi varieties ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Before we proceed, we must comment on the inclusion <math id="S6.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\hookrightarrow V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\hookrightarrow V</annotation></semantics></math>. The complement consists of <math id="S6.p14.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="([\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}],v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">([\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}],v)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in H^{0}(C,\alpha^{*}O(m))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in H^{0}(C,\alpha^{*}O(m))</annotation></semantics></math> does not give a birational map to <math id="S6.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{F}}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐅</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{F}}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us disregard the <math id="S6.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>’s that factor non-trivially (such <math id="S6.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>’s form set of codimension at least two). Then the only such <math id="S6.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> are the pullbacks of the sections in <math id="S6.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}({\bf P}^{1},O(m))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}({\bf P}^{1},O(m))</annotation></semantics></math>. The locus <math id="S6.p14.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="([\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}],v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">([\alpha{\colon}C\to{\bf P}^{1}],v)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in\alpha^{*}H^{0}({\bf P}^{1},O(m))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in\alpha^{*}H^{0}({\bf P}^{1},O(m))</annotation></semantics></math> has codimension at least two except in the case <math id="S6.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\equiv-1\pmod{(d-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo rspace="0em">≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">mod</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\equiv-1\pmod{(d-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=\lfloor(g+d-1)/(d-1)\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=\lfloor(g+d-1)/(d-1)\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, when the generic splitting of <math id="S6.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{*}O_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{*}O_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{*}O_{C}=O\oplus O(-m)\oplus O(-m-1)\oplus\dots\oplus O(-m-1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{*}O_{C}=O\oplus O(-m)\oplus O(-m-1)\oplus\dots\oplus O(-m-1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">In this case, the complement of <math id="S6.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S6.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> has a divisorial component given by the image of the constant vector bundle <math id="S6.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}({\bf P}^{1},O(m))\otimes O_{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}({\bf P}^{1},O(m))\otimes O_{W}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the class of this divisor in <math id="S6.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}V\cong\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}V\cong\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}W</annotation></semantics></math> is in the span of <math id="S6.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p14.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p14.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, in any case, <math id="S6.p14.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}V</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by <math id="S6.p14.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p14.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p14.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S6.p14.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}X</annotation></semantics></math> is.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume that <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="6.1. ‣ 6 From Hurwitz spaces to Severi varieties ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> holds. From diagram (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 6 From Hurwitz spaces to Severi varieties ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>), we see that <math id="S6.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}X</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by <math id="S6.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>. By the comment about <math id="S6.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\hookrightarrow V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\hookrightarrow V</annotation></semantics></math> above, this implies that <math id="S6.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}V</annotation></semantics></math>, and in turn <math id="S6.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}^{\dagger}_{d,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>†</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\widetilde{\mathcal{H}}^{\dagger}_{d,g}</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by <math id="S6.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem5" title="6.5. ‣ 6 From Hurwitz spaces to Severi varieties ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a> holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume that <math id="S6.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\geq\lceil 2(g+d-1)/(d-1)\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\geq\lceil 2(g+d-1)/(d-1)\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> and <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem5" title="6.5. ‣ 6 From Hurwitz spaces to Severi varieties ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a> holds. Then, by <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="2.6. ‣ 2.1 The associated scroll construction ‣ 2 The Maroni and Casnati–Ekedahl loci ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a> the inclusion <math id="S6.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\hookrightarrow\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\hookrightarrow\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is in fact an isomorphism. Again, diagram (<a href="#S6.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 6 From Hurwitz spaces to Severi varieties ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>) shows that <math id="S6.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Pic</mi><mi>𝐐</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{Pic}_{\bf Q}\widetilde{\mathcal{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by <math id="S6.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="6.1. ‣ 6 From Hurwitz spaces to Severi varieties ‣ The Picard rank conjecture for the Hurwitz spaces of degree up to five" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a> holds.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We are grateful to our advisor Joe Harris for suggesting this problem
and for providing constant support and encouragement. We also thank
Gabriel Bujokas, Dawei Chen, Maksym Fedorchuk, and Ravi Vakil for
helpful comments and conversations.</p>
</div>
</section>
